   "source": [
    "### RQ2 Statistical Interpretation\n",
    "\n",
    "The Control group had a Step Back rate of 26.10%, while the Test group had a Step Back rate of 33.41%.\n",
    "\n",
    "The Test group therefore showed an absolute increase of 7.31 percentage points and a relative increase of 28.02% in Step Back behavior.\n",
    "\n",
    "A one tailed two proportion Z test produced a Z statistic of 17.8884. At an alpha level of 0.05, the critical value was 1.6449.\n",
    "\n",
    "Because the test statistic exceeded the critical value and the p value was below 0.05, the null hypothesis was rejected.\n",
    "\n",
    "The 95% confidence interval for the difference in Step Back rates ranged from 6.52 to 8.11 percentage points. Since the entire interval is above zero, the result supports a higher Step Back rate in the Test group.\n",
    "\n",
    "Cohen's h was 0.1602, indicating an effect below the conventional small effect threshold of 0.20.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Decision\n",
    "\n",
    "Reject H0.\n",
    "\n",
    "There is statistically significant evidence that the redesigned digital process increased the Step Back rate compared with the existing process.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Business Interpretation\n",
    "\n",
    "The redesigned process increased the Step Back rate from 26.10% to 33.41%, representing an increase of 7.31 percentage points.\n",
    "\n",
    "Although the statistical effect size is below the conventional small effect threshold, the 28.02% relative increase in Step Back behavior is operationally relevant. More clients returning to a previous step may indicate additional review, correction, uncertainty, or friction within the redesigned process.\n",
    "\n",
    "This result should therefore be considered alongside completion rate, completion time, error behavior, and funnel performance before making the final recommendation on the redesigned process."
   "id": "139c5e3c-5583-4baf-8ae9-0848a5bc402b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### How much faster is it, really?\n",
    "\n",
    "-> Test clients are about 34 seconds faster (0.57 min × 60), and the interval [-0.65, -0.48] is entirely below zero, so the direction isn't in doubt.\n",
    "\n",
    "-> 34 seconds sounds small, but the Control median is 4.52 minutes, so 0.57 / 4.52 = roughly **13% faster**. That's the version worth putting in front of Vanguard.\n",
    "\n",
    "-> Note the point estimate (-0.57) comes from the real data. Only the interval comes from the simulation -> the bootstrap measures uncertainty, it doesn't estimate the value."
   ]
